<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jaffa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yafo, Joppa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jaffa is an ancient port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of Israel, now part of Tel Aviv–Yafo. Long the main sea gateway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, it was taken by the crusaders in 1099 and became the centre of the County of Jaffa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> won a battle there in 1192, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> captured it in 1268. Tel Aviv was founded on its outskirts in 1909.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jaffa.jpg" alt="Jaffa" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Godot13 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISR-2013-Aerial-Jaffa-Port_of_Jaffa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The great lordships, among them Galilee, <b>Jaffa</b> and Ascalon, Sidon and Oultrejordain, held extensive rights over their lands and subjects.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Riots in Jerusalem in April 1920 killed five Jews and four Arabs; in May 1921 almost 100 died in <b>Jaffa</b> after clashes between rival Jewish protesters and then Arab attacks on Jews; in August 1929 riots in which 250 people died included the Hebron massacre of Jews.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Such service had its reward: for supporting Cambyses II's conquest of Egypt the Persian king granted Sidon Dor, <b>Joppa</b> and the Plain of Sharon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>French troops took the port of <b>Jaffa</b> during Napoleon's Syrian campaign.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-7">7 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Shortly after the Battle of <b>Jaffa</b>, Richard I of England and Saladin, Sultan of Egypt, agreed a three-year truce, negotiated with the help of Balian of Ibelin.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-2">2 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The victory let the crusaders take <b>Jaffa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-7">7 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
